-This will end the request with `400 - Bad Request` status code and the following JSON response:
+This will end the request with `400 - Bad Request` status code and the following JSON response, sent as `application/problem+json` ([RFC 9457](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457) problem details):
 
 ```json
 {
-  "date": "2025-06-05T04:20:00.0Z",
-  "status": 400,
   "statusText": "Bad Request",
   "message": "Invalid user input",
   "data": {
     "field": "email"
-  }
+  },
+  "date": "2025-06-05T04:20:00.0Z",
+  "type": "about:blank",
+  "title": "Bad Request",
+  "status": 400,
+  "detail": "Invalid user input"
 }
 ```
 
+`type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` and `instance` are the standard RFC 9457 members and cannot be overridden by `body`. `statusText`, `message`, `data` and the `body` properties are kept as extension members.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> Error responses use the `application/problem+json` content-type instead of `application/json`. Clients comparing the header for exact equality must be updated. A custom `content-type` set via `headers` takes precedence. An `onError` hook that returns its own response is not formatted automatically.
+
 ### `HTTPError` Fields
 
 - `status`: HTTP status code in the range 200–599.
@@ -56,6 +64,8 @@ This will end the request with `400 - Bad Request` status code and the following
 - `data`: Additional data to be attached under the `data` key in the error JSON body.
 - `body`: Additional top-level properties to be attached in the error JSON body.
 - `headers`: Additional HTTP headers to be sent in the error response.
+- `type`: URI reference identifying the problem type. Defaults to `"about:blank"` in the response.
+- `instance`: URI reference identifying this specific occurrence. Omitted from the response when not set.
 - `cause`: The original error object that caused this error, useful for tracing and debugging.
 - `unhandled`: Indicates whether the error was thrown for unknown reasons. See [Unhandled Errors](#unhandled-errors).
 
